# T0011: Modern dashboard design research for CATE (home, models page, top-right theme control)

**Short version:** CATE should look **calm, neutral and dense**, in the style of Linear and Vercel. That means near-neutral grey surfaces with a slight tint, one accent colour (keep CATE's teal), Inter type with tabular numbers, separation by borders and surface shade instead of heavy shadows, and smaller corner radii. The changes with the most impact:

1. **A shared top bar on every page.** Page title on the left. On the right: usage ring, **theme (brightness) button** and sign-out. The sidebar footer and the "show archived" button go away.
2. **Models page rebuilt around "Add a model or API".** A visible 3-step guide and a primary button at the top. Short connection cards, with model and role settings folded away until needed. An optional "team map" node diagram (supervisor → connections → models).
3. **Home page in "composer first" style** like the big chat apps, with a team-status strip and recent projects under it. This follows NN/g's empty-state guidance: show system status and give direct routes into key tasks.
4. **Fix contrast.** I measured today's colours and found real WCAG failures (table below). New tokens that pass are included.
5. **Lists continue in the composer.** Shift+Enter after `1. ` or `- ` adds the next number or bullet. I wrote a small helper and tested it in Node (results below).

All CSS below uses CATE's **existing class and variable names** (`web/app/globals.css`), so it can go in piece by piece.

---

## 1. What I checked (sources)

| Source | What I took from it |
|---|---|
| [Linear – How we redesigned the Linear UI](https://linear.app/now/how-we-redesigned-the-linear-ui) | Themes are generated in **LCH** from 3 inputs (base colour, accent colour, contrast) instead of 98 hand-set variables. Blue tint was limited for a "more neutral and timeless appearance". **Inter Display for headings, Inter for body.** Sidebar and tabs were changed to "reduce visual noise… increase the hierarchy and density". Surfaces get elevation (background → panel → dialog → modal) from lightness steps. |
| [Radix Colors – Understanding the scale](https://www.radix-ui.com/colors/docs/palette-composition/understanding-the-scale) | A 12-step role scale: 1–2 app and subtle backgrounds; 3–5 component normal, hover and selected; 6–8 borders (subtle, interactive, strong/focus); 9–10 solid and solid-hover; 11–12 low- and high-contrast text. |
| [Vercel Geist – Colors](https://vercel.com/geist/colors.md) | The same idea in 10 steps: 100–300 backgrounds (default, hover, active), 400–600 borders, 700–800 high-contrast backgrounds, 900 secondary text, 1000 primary text. Two page backgrounds (Background 1 and 2). |
| [Chrome – Dark mode toggles guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/ui-components/dark-mode-toggle) and [Lea Verou – Dark mode toggles: two states are enough](https://lea.verou.me/blog/2026/dark-mode-toggles/) | A toggle in the header should be **two-state**: show the opposite of what is on screen. Pressing it again when it matches the OS **removes** the stored choice, so the page goes back to "System". Light/Dark/System as three options belongs in a settings panel. Apply the saved value before first paint with a tiny inline script. Wrap `localStorage` in try/catch. Sync tabs with the `storage` event. |
| [shadcn/ui – Dark mode (Next.js)](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/dark-mode/next) | The common pattern: `next-themes`, `defaultTheme="system"`, `enableSystem`, `disableTransitionOnChange`, `suppressHydrationWarning` on `<html>`. The toggle is a Sun/Moon icon button in the header. |
| [NN/g – Dark mode: users & issues](https://nngroup.com/articles/dark-mode-users-issues) | Users who set dark mode in their OS expect sites to follow it automatically. |
| [NN/g – Empty states in complex applications](https://nngroup.com/articles/empty-state-interface-design) | Empty states should show system status, help people learn the system, and give direct routes into key tasks. Never say "nothing here" while data is still loading. |
| [Open WebUI – connect a provider](https://docs.openwebui.com/getting-started/quick-start/starting-with-openai) | How a mature multi-model chat app does "add a model": **Admin → Settings → Connections → "+"**, enter URL and key, then it fetches the model list. It recommends an **allowlist** so the model picker does not get cluttered. |
| [LobeHub – New AI provider management (changelog, Jan 2025)](https://lobehub.com/changelog/2025-01-22-new-ai-provider) | A providers list where you can add, edit or remove providers and add custom models with capability settings. A third-party guide (not official) says the "+" sits at the top right of the provider list. |
| [OpenRouter models catalogue](https://openrouter.ai/models?fmt=cards) | Model catalogue cards show developer icon, model name, usage and a one-line description, with search, filter and sort. I could only read the page as text, so this is about content, not visual style. |
| [React Flow examples](https://reactflow.dev/examples) (MIT, `@xyflow/react`) | Free examples: custom nodes, node toolbar, edge label renderer, edge markers, animated edges, dagre/elk tree layout. Auto-layout, shapes and edge routing are **Pro** (paid). |
| [n8n new canvas (community post)](https://community.n8n.io/t/help-us-test-the-new-n8n-canvas-beta/60070) | Modern node canvases are **flat**: no 3D shadows, plus a minimap for big graphs. Users later complained about low contrast on the canvas, so check contrast on nodes too. |
| [W3C – Understanding SC 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum) and [WebAIM contrast](https://webaim.org/articles/contrast/) | Normal text needs **4.5:1**. Large text (≥18pt, or ≥14pt bold) needs 3:1. Non-text UI such as icons and input outlines that identify a control needs 3:1 (SC 1.4.11). |
| [Inter (rsms.me)](https://rsms.me/inter/) and [CoinGecko brand typography](https://brand.coingecko.com/visual-identity/typography/primary-typeface) | Use **tabular figures** (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`) for metrics and tables so numbers line up. |
| [Evil Martians – OKLCH in CSS](https://evilmartians.com/chronicles/oklch-in-css-why-quit-rgb-hsl) | OKLCH has perceptually even lightness, which makes it good for generating scales. Still check contrast with real measurements; even lightness alone does not guarantee contrast. |
| [MDN – light-dark()](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/CSS/color_value/light-dark) | Baseline since May 2024 (Chrome/Edge 123, Firefox 120, Safari 17.5). Needs `color-scheme: light dark`. Optional; CATE's `[data-theme]` variables already work. |
| [Vercel – Geist font](https://vercel.com/font/geist) | `import { Geist, Geist_Mono } from 'next/font/google'` works. Vercel notes the Google Fonts build has only **partial** glyph and font-feature support; the npm `geist` package has the full set. |

**Limits of this research:** I read these pages as text and could not view screenshots, so I describe layouts by their structure, not by pixel detail. I did not use any Pro or paid templates. The visual descriptions of ChatGPT, Claude and Perplexity home screens below are general patterns, not claims about any one release.

---

## 2. Patterns that make dashboards look "professional"

1. **Neutral first, one accent.** Grey surfaces with a tiny hue tint. The accent appears only on the primary action, focus rings, selected state and progress. Linear deliberately cut back blue chrome. CATE's teal can stay, but use it less: today it is on the avatar, banners, chips, plan border and meters all at once.
2. **Elevation by lightness, not shadow (dark mode).** App background < sidebar < card < popover. Hairline borders (Radix steps 6–7) separate areas. Keep shadows only for floating layers: popovers, menus and dialogs.
3. **Role-based tokens.** Name colours by job (bg, surface, hover, border, border-strong, text, text-muted). Geist and Radix both do this, and CATE already half does. Adding the missing `hover`, `selected` and `line-strong` roles fixes most "looks a bit off" states.
4. **Density with hierarchy.** 13–14px UI text, 4px spacing grid, 32–36px control height, section labels in small caps or 12px muted. Headings weight 600 with slightly tighter letter-spacing.
5. **Smaller, consistent radii.** 6px for small controls, 8px for buttons and inputs, 12px for cards and popovers. CATE uses 14–18px cards plus pill buttons, which reads as "consumer app" rather than "pro tool".
6. **Rectangular buttons with clear levels.** Primary (solid accent), secondary (surface + border), ghost (no border), danger (red text). Use pills only for status badges.
7. **Progressive disclosure.** Show status and the one key number up front. Put configuration behind "Configure" or `<details>`. This is how Open WebUI and LobeChat keep provider pages readable.
8. **Status at a glance.** Coloured dot + word ("Connected", "Paused", "Not connected"), never colour alone.
9. **Tabular numbers** in meters, percentages, token counts and tables.
10. **Header holds global controls.** Theme, account and global status sit at the top right. This is the shadcn/Vercel-style layout the user asked for.

---

## 3. Contrast audit of the current palette (measured)

I computed WCAG 2 contrast ratios for colours in `web/app/globals.css`:

| Pair (current) | Ratio | Needed | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dark `--faint #6e7880` on `--surface #171b1e` | **3.85** | 4.5 | ❌ fails (used for meta text, notes, table headers) |
| Dark `--faint` on `--bg #0f1214` | 4.17 | 4.5 | ❌ |
| Dark `--muted #a0a9b0` on `--surface` | 7.26 | 4.5 | ✅ |
| Light `--faint #8a949b` on white | **3.09** | 4.5 | ❌ |
| Light `--faint` on `--surface #f3f5f5` | **2.83** | 4.5 | ❌ |
| Light `--accent #0d9488` as link text on white | **3.74** | 4.5 | ❌ |
| Light white text on `--accent` (primary button) | **3.74** | 4.5 | ❌ for 13.5px button text |
| Dark `--line #2a3136` vs `--surface` | 1.31 | (3:1 only when the border alone identifies a control) | ⚠️ fine for dividers, too weak for input outlines |

The proposed tokens in §4 were measured the same way (selected values):

| Pair (proposed) | Ratio |
|---|---|
| Dark ink `#eceff1` on bg `#0b0d0f` | 16.9 |
| Dark muted `#a7b0b6` on surface `#15191b` | 8.0 |
| Dark faint `#8a949a` on surface / surface-2 / bg | 5.7 / 5.3 / 6.3 |
| Dark accent `#2dd4bf` on surface; accent-ink `#04201d` on accent | 9.5 / 9.2 |
| Dark ok / warn / bad on surface | 9.2 / 10.6 / 6.4 |
| Dark input border `#5b666c` on bg / surface | 3.3 / 3.0 |
| Light ink `#101416` on `#fafafa` | 17.8 |
| Light muted `#4b555c` on white | 7.6 |
| Light faint `#646e75` on white / `#fafafa` / surface-2 `#f1f3f4` | 5.2 / 5.0 / 4.7 |
| Light accent text `#0f766e` on white; white on `#0f766e` button | 5.5 / 5.5 |
| Light ok `#047857` / warn `#b45309` / bad `#b91c1c` on white | 5.5 / 5.0 / 6.5 |

---

## 4. Design tokens: drop-in replacement for the top of `globals.css`

Variable names match what CATE uses today. New roles (`--hover`, `--selected`, `--line-strong`, `--accent-solid`, `--accent-text`, radii, type) are additions. Before switching all link and button styles over, alias `--accent-text` to `--accent` in dark mode so nothing breaks.

```css
:root {
  color-scheme: dark;
  /* surfaces: elevation by lightness */
  --bg: #0b0d0f;          /* app background (Radix 1 / Geist bg-100) */
  --panel: #0f1214;       /* sidebar, top bar */
  --surface: #15191b;     /* cards (Radix 2) */
  --surface-2: #1c2124;   /* inputs, hover on cards (Radix 3) */
  --hover: #22282b;       /* hovered row / nav item (Radix 4) */
  --selected: #283033;    /* current nav item, selected row (Radix 5) */
  --raised: #1d2326;      /* popovers, menus */
  /* borders */
  --line-soft: #1f2528;   /* dividers inside cards (Radix 6) */
  --line: #2a3134;        /* card / section borders (Radix 7) */
  --line-strong: #5b666c; /* input outlines, ≥3:1 (Radix 8) */
  /* text */
  --ink: #eceff1;
  --muted: #a7b0b6;
  --faint: #8a949a;       /* was #6e7880: failed 4.5:1 */
  /* accent: teal kept */
  --accent: #2dd4bf;
  --accent-solid: #2dd4bf;
  --accent-text: #2dd4bf;
  --accent-ink: #04201d;
  --accent-soft: rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.10);
  --accent-line: rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.32);
  /* status */
  --ok: #34d399; --warn: #fbbf24; --bad: #f87171;
  --ok-soft: rgba(52, 211, 153, .12); --warn-soft: rgba(251, 191, 36, .12); --bad-soft: rgba(248, 113, 113, .12);
  --user: #17201f;
  /* shape + depth */
  --r-sm: 6px; --r-md: 8px; --r-lg: 12px;
  --shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, .45), 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, .04);
  /* type */
  --fs-xs: 12px; --fs-sm: 13px; --fs-md: 14px; --fs-lg: 16px; --fs-xl: 20px; --fs-2xl: 26px;
}

:root[data-theme="light"] {
  color-scheme: light;
  --bg: #fafafa;
  --panel: #f5f6f6;
  --surface: #ffffff;
  --surface-2: #f1f3f4;
  --hover: #eceff0;
  --selected: #e4e8ea;
  --raised: #ffffff;
  --line-soft: #eceff0;
  --line: #e1e5e7;
  --line-strong: #8c969c;  /* 3.0:1 on white */
  --ink: #101416;
  --muted: #4b555c;
  --faint: #646e75;        /* was #8a949b: 3.1:1 */
  --accent: #0d9488;       /* fills, meters, focus ring only */
  --accent-solid: #0f766e; /* primary button background: white text 5.5:1 */
  --accent-text: #0f766e;  /* links and accent text on white: 5.5:1 */
  --accent-ink: #ffffff;
  --accent-soft: rgba(13, 148, 136, .09);
  --accent-line: rgba(13, 148, 136, .32);
  --ok: #047857; --warn: #b45309; --bad: #b91c1c;
  --ok-soft: rgba(4, 120, 87, .08); --warn-soft: rgba(180, 83, 9, .08); --bad-soft: rgba(185, 28, 28, .07);
  --user: #eef7f6;
  --shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(16, 20, 22, .08), 0 0 0 1px rgba(16, 20, 22, .05);
}

body { font-size: var(--fs-md); line-height: 1.5; }
h1, h2, h3 { letter-spacing: -0.012em; font-weight: 600; }
.num, .limit b, .table td, .pill, .meter-label { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.link, .prose a { color: var(--accent-text); }

/* buttons: rectangular, three levels */
.button { border-radius: var(--r-md); height: 32px; padding: 0 12px; font-weight: 550; border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--surface); }
.button:hover { background: var(--hover); border-color: var(--line-strong); }
.button.primary { background: var(--accent-solid); border-color: var(--accent-solid); color: var(--accent-ink); }
.button.primary:hover { filter: brightness(1.06); }
.button.ghost { background: transparent; border-color: transparent; }
.button.ghost:hover { background: var(--hover); }
.button.small { height: 28px; padding: 0 10px; font-size: var(--fs-sm); }
.card { border-radius: var(--r-lg); border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--surface); }
.field input, .field select, .field textarea, .select { border-color: var(--line-strong); border-radius: var(--r-md); }
.nav-item:hover, .chat-link:hover { background: var(--hover); }
.nav-item[aria-current="page"], .chat-link[aria-current="page"] { background: var(--selected); color: var(--ink); }
.pill.ok { background: var(--ok-soft); } .pill.warn { background: var(--warn-soft); } .pill.bad { background: var(--bad-soft); }
```

Notes:
- **Font:** keep **Inter**, which `app/layout.js` already loads. Linear uses Inter for body and Inter Display for headings. If you want a more "Vercel" feel, Geist is available via `next/font/google` (`Geist`, `Geist_Mono`). Vercel says the Google build has partial glyph and feature support; use the npm `geist` package if that matters. Switching fonts is optional; the colour, radius and density changes matter more.
- **Generating scales later:** to let users pick their own accent (Linear-style "base + accent + contrast"), generate the steps in OKLCH and measure contrast after generation.

---

## 5. Top bar with the brightness (theme) control at top right

### Layout
```
┌ sidebar ─────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ CATE             │ ☰  Models & connections                 ◔ 62%   ☀︎   ⎋        │  ← .topbar (52px, sticky)
│ + New project    ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Chat             │                                                              │
│ Models           │   page content                                               │
│ Logs             │                                                              │
│ ── Today ──      │                                                              │
│ • Project A      │                                                              │
└──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
- One `.topbar` for every page, replacing `.chat-head` and the `page-head` + `MenuButton` combination. The left side holds the menu button on mobile and the page or project title. The right side holds the **usage ring** (moved from the composer, which also clears space there), the **theme button** and sign-out.
- **Remove `.side-foot`.** It only held archive, theme and sign-out. Removing the archive toggle and `archived` state matches "show archived projects is not required right now". The archive action on a single project can stay.

### Behaviour (from Chrome's guidance and Lea Verou)
- Icon button with **two states**: it shows what you will switch to (sun when dark, moon when light), with `aria-label="Switch to light theme"`.
- First visit follows the OS (`prefers-color-scheme`). Today CATE always starts dark and *always* stores the choice, so users can never get back to "follow system".
- On click: target = the opposite of what is on screen. If the target equals the OS setting, **remove** `cate-theme`; otherwise store it. Then CATE follows the OS again.
- Keep the existing pre-paint inline script in `app/layout.js` so the page does not flash. Listen for `storage` so other open tabs update too.
- Optional: a small popover under the button offering Light · Dark · System (with "System (currently dark)"). Chrome's guidance puts three-way choices in a settings surface, so keep the one-click button as the main control.
- *Interpretation note:* I read "brightness operator bar" as the light/dark theme control. If the owner meant a brightness *slider*, the same top-right slot works for a slider popover. A CSS `filter: brightness()` on `.main` is not recommended because it dims images and text unevenly and hurts contrast.

### Code sketch
```js
// components/ThemeButton.js
'use client';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Moon, Sun } from '@/components/Icons';

const KEY = 'cate-theme';
const systemDark = () => window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
const apply = (t) => { document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t; };

export default function ThemeButton() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');
  useEffect(() => {
    setTheme(document.documentElement.dataset.theme || 'dark');
    const mq = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
    const sync = () => {
      let saved = null; try { saved = localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch {}
      const t = saved || (mq.matches ? 'dark' : 'light'); apply(t); setTheme(t);
    };
    mq.addEventListener('change', sync); window.addEventListener('storage', sync);
    return () => { mq.removeEventListener('change', sync); window.removeEventListener('storage', sync); };
  }, []);
  const flip = () => {
    const next = theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
    try {
      if ((next === 'dark') === systemDark()) localStorage.removeItem(KEY); // back to "follow system"
      else localStorage.setItem(KEY, next);
    } catch {}
    apply(next); setTheme(next);
  };
  const label = theme === 'dark' ? 'Switch to light theme' : 'Switch to dark theme';
  return <button className="icon-btn" onClick={flip} aria-label={label} title={label}>{theme === 'dark' ? <Sun /> : <Moon />}</button>;
}
```
Pre-paint script in `app/layout.js`. It replaces the current one, which only handles `light`:
```js
const themeScript = `try{var t=localStorage.getItem('cate-theme')||(matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches?'dark':'light');document.documentElement.dataset.theme=t}catch(e){}`;
```
(If the owner wants CATE to stay **dark by default** whatever the OS says, keep `'dark'` as the fallback instead of `matchMedia`. That is a product choice.)

```css
